WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.320
This video is sponsored by the Great Courses Plus

00:00:03.600 --> 00:00:06.760
Stories are in, under, and behind almost everything.

00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.880
Our identities, our beliefs, our friendships and families,  our societies, and so on

00:00:12.000 --> 00:00:15.060
Stories have a way of not only entertaining, but training us.

00:00:15.260 --> 00:00:18.620
They can be incredibly powerful and beautiful devices that form and assist

00:00:18.620 --> 00:00:20.720
our perception and understanding of the world.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:24.515
However, according to twentieth century American author Kurt Vonnegut

00:00:24.515 --> 00:00:26.945
Stories rarely tell the truth.

00:00:26.945 --> 00:00:29.860
After studying stories from an anthropological standpoint,

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:32.080
examining their relationships with various cultures,

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:35.080
Vonnegut found that stories and myths across many cultures

00:00:35.140 --> 00:00:40.200
share consistent similar shapes that can typically be broken down into just a few main categories

00:00:40.200 --> 00:00:44.100
These shapes can be found by graphing the course of a protagonist's journey through a story

00:00:44.105 --> 00:00:47.155
along an axis of good and ill fortune.

00:00:47.155 --> 00:00:50.355
In all stories someone or something starts somewhere

00:00:50.360 --> 00:00:53.200
Either in a good place, bad place, or neutral place.

00:00:53.200 --> 00:00:57.020
Then things happen related to that person which are conveyed as good or bad

00:00:57.020 --> 00:01:01.640
bringing the character up and down the axis of fortune as they traverse forward through the story.

00:01:01.640 --> 00:01:04.620
Then, the story ends and its shape reveals itself

00:01:04.620 --> 00:01:11.020
Vonnegut discovered that many popular stories follow common, consistent curves and spikes up and down the good/ill fortune axis

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:14.620
and that most end with the protagonist higher on the axis than where they started.

00:01:14.620 --> 00:01:17.700
However, what's perhaps most interesting about Vonnegut's analysis

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:22.260
is his argument that these shapes, and consequently most stories, lie.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:27.200
Vonnegut proposed that a more honest, realistic story shape is simply a straight line.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:31.180
In a story of this shape, things still happen and characters still change

00:01:31.180 --> 00:01:36.940
but the story maintains ambiguity around whether or not the events that occur are conclusively good or bad.

00:01:36.940 --> 00:01:42.480
One example of a story that Vonnegut references with this straight line shape is Shakespeare's play Hamlet

00:01:42.480 --> 00:01:47.020
A play that has and continues to be regarded by many as one of the best and most famous plays,

00:01:47.100 --> 00:01:49.040
and perhaps literary works in general,

00:01:49.040 --> 00:01:50.580
of all time

00:01:50.640 --> 00:01:55.560
In which the story moves forward and things happen but none of the events are conclusively good or bad

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.520
and thus the audience is left unaware as to what is.

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.660
Which, according to Vonnegut, is the closest literary representation of real life

00:02:03.220 --> 00:02:05.520
We are so seldom told the truth

00:02:05.540 --> 00:02:11.240
In Hamlet - in Hamlet, Shakespeare tells us

00:02:11.240 --> 00:02:14.200
We don't know enough about life to know

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:17.335
what the good news is and the bad news is

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:18.720
and we respond to that

00:02:19.280 --> 00:02:22.420
One story medium that seems to inadvertently coincide with this idea

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:25.120
is the medium of the television series

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:28.580
The goal of a TV series is to keep viewers watching as long as possible.

00:02:28.580 --> 00:02:32.820
Each episode must be an engaging enough story to keep the viewer watching until the end

00:02:32.840 --> 00:02:35.500
but each episode must also be left unresolved enough

00:02:35.500 --> 00:02:38.380
so the larger season-long and series-long stories continue

00:02:38.380 --> 00:02:41.280
and the viewer is interested in watching all the following episodes.

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:43.860
If enough people watch all the episodes in a season

00:02:43.900 --> 00:02:45.640
the show gets renewed for more seasons

00:02:45.640 --> 00:02:48.420
and the cycle continues for as many seasons as possible.

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:51.780
In order to keep the whole thing going, none of the stories can reach a conclusion

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:57.860
and thus, the main characters can't find ultimate peace or freedom from the uncertainty between good and ill fortune.

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:01.880
Of course, most shows don't qualify as the straight line shape in Vonnegut's analysis

00:03:01.880 --> 00:03:05.920
because most shows attempt to convey conclusively good and bad fortunes within them.

00:03:05.920 --> 00:03:08.900
However, merely by the requirements of the medium

00:03:08.900 --> 00:03:13.580
TV series are forced to self-impose the same sort of universal truth that Vonnegut suggests.

00:03:13.620 --> 00:03:15.880
That neither the viewer nor the characters in a series

00:03:15.880 --> 00:03:19.360
can ever know what anything that's so called 'good' or 'bad' in one episode

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:20.740
might cause in the next.

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:22.400
And that on a fundamental level

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:27.200
the changes in each episode are futile because they are a part of a never-ending cycle of change

00:03:27.200 --> 00:03:28.780
through conflict and resolution

00:03:28.780 --> 00:03:30.800
for the mere sake of its continuation

00:03:30.800 --> 00:03:35.220
with no aim of final resolution or reveal of what's ultimately good or bad.

00:03:35.220 --> 00:03:37.900
Of course, eventually a show reaches its series end

00:03:37.900 --> 00:03:40.240
when it stops working or runs its natural course.

00:03:40.360 --> 00:03:43.180
But the show fights its whole life to stay away from this moment.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:46.080
A good TV series, a series that we don't want to end,

00:03:46.080 --> 00:03:50.080
is only a series that we don't want to end because it can't seem to resolve itself.

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:54.180
In this, the format of a TV series also shows us that there is meaning,

00:03:54.240 --> 00:03:57.160
engagement, and entertainment within the endless cycle of change

00:03:57.160 --> 00:04:00.360
regardless of its potential universal futility.

00:04:00.360 --> 00:04:02.360
And that perhaps change in life can exist

00:04:02.360 --> 00:04:05.420
not for the sake of some conclusion or ultimate state of peace

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:07.880
but a continuation of itself for the sake of itself.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:12.780
A continual adaptation and movement in life, so to keep life alive and interesting.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:16.820
And perhaps the ability to be in this cycle of continued change for the sake of change

00:04:16.820 --> 00:04:18.260
is the actual good fortune.

00:04:18.440 --> 00:04:23.220
Renowned twentieth century mythologist, writer, and professor of literature Joseph Campbell said:

00:04:23.220 --> 00:04:26.380
What I think is that a good life is one hero journey after another.

00:04:26.380 --> 00:04:30.180
Over and over again you are called to the realm of adventure,

00:04:30.180 --> 00:04:31.560
you are called to new horizons.

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:33.600
Each time there is the same problem:

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:34.560
Do I dare?

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:36.940
And then if you do dare, the dangers are there

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:38.200
and the help also

00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:40.160
and the fulfillment or the fiasco.

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:44.800
Stories help us see and understand various micro-moments and aspects of life.

00:04:44.800 --> 00:04:48.520
They help us better connect and share ideas, lessons, and meaning.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:51.060
They are a beautiful, essential device of conscious life.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:54.580
And stories don't always have to be real or accurately representative of life

00:04:54.580 --> 00:04:56.320
to be helpful and important.

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:01.040
But the truth is we appear to so often see life the same way that our favorite stories do.

00:05:01.040 --> 00:05:05.860
It is no coincidence that most good stories share the same basic structure, ingredients, and shapes.

00:05:05.860 --> 00:05:08.060
They're a reflection of how we often like to think

00:05:08.060 --> 00:05:10.080
and how we often want the world to be.

00:05:10.080 --> 00:05:14.260
However, when we assume that our entire life can fit neatly into the templates of our favorite stories,

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:18.760
or the Hollywood films we are so often inclined to believe and try to model our life after,

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:22.620
when we expect or pretend to always know what ultimately good or bad fortune is

00:05:22.620 --> 00:05:26.500
we create an expectation in every decision, action, and event

00:05:26.500 --> 00:05:28.020
that carries so much pressure

00:05:28.020 --> 00:05:31.260
we are likely to fear any decision or action at all.

00:05:31.260 --> 00:05:36.600
Perhaps the nineteenth century highly influential philosopher Søren Kierkegaard said it best when he said:

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:40.680
Life can only be understood backwards, but it must be lived forwards.

00:05:40.980 --> 00:05:43.060
This unknown can be rather terrifying

00:05:43.060 --> 00:05:47.480
but the fear is only intensified to an unnecessarily immobile state of paralysis

00:05:47.480 --> 00:05:49.080
when we maintain the assumption

00:05:49.080 --> 00:05:52.100
that there can ever be a perfect, right decision in anything

00:05:52.100 --> 00:05:55.360
that will lead to an ultimately noble resolution of everything.

00:05:55.480 --> 00:05:59.460
Rather, we should perhaps work to simply have the fortitude to make decisions at all

00:05:59.460 --> 00:06:01.440
to forge onward through our story

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:04.520
trying our best to navigate with decency, effort, and honesty

00:06:04.640 --> 00:06:07.000
Accepting whatever our decisions might cause

00:06:07.000 --> 00:06:08.940
and whatever events might occur onto us

00:06:08.980 --> 00:06:11.600
And understanding that so long as we are still alive

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:14.540
our story will go on and maintain the qualities of all stories:

00:06:14.560 --> 00:06:18.360
an opportunity for further experience, adaptation, and triumph.

00:06:18.520 --> 00:06:22.020
Even though we can't know what the good or bad news is within the bigger picture

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:23.960
Vonnegut believed that we don't need to know

00:06:23.960 --> 00:06:27.080
to be able to look around and know when things are nice amidst it all

00:06:27.140 --> 00:06:30.860
To recognize the happy moments in our life, however big or small

00:06:30.980 --> 00:06:31.960
and stop and say:

00:06:33.560 --> 00:06:36.640
If this isn't nice, I don't know what is. Music, please!

